**Goal** — Move the body of `AgentManager.completeAsWithFallback` into `completeAsWithFallback(input, agentName, prompt, options)` in a new module, leaving a one-line delegation, with no behaviour change.

**Motivation** — `packages/nax/src/agents/manager.ts` is at exactly 600 lines (the source limit, no `file-sizes-baseline.json` entry), so any net growth fails `check:file-sizes`; this frees the budget US-002 and US-003 need.

**Approach** — Refactor only. `AgentManager.completeAsWithFallback(agentName, prompt, options)` (about lines 512-566 of manager.ts) currently builds the complete-call preamble, awaits `this.completeWithFallback`, and emits the dispatch event or the dispatch-error event. The body moves unchanged into `completeAsWithFallback(input, agentName, prompt, options)` in `packages/nax/src/agents/manager-complete-as.ts`. `input` is `{ config, dispatchEvents, completeWithFallback }`. The method stays on `AgentManager` as a one-line delegation with the same signature. This mirrors how `runWithFallback` lives in `manager-run-fallback.ts`. Verification: `bun run typecheck` and `bun run check:all` in `packages/nax` pass; `check:file-sizes` reports `src/agents/manager.ts` at or below 565 lines; the existing `completeAs` and `completeAsWithFallback` tests (for example `manager-complete.test.ts`) pass unchanged.

**Scope** — In: new `manager-complete-as.ts`, one-line delegation in `manager.ts`. Out: any cooldown/auth logic (US-002, US-003); any signature change on `AgentManager`.

**Interface**
```ts
// packages/nax/src/agents/manager-complete-as.ts
export async function completeAsWithFallback(
  input: { config; dispatchEvents; completeWithFallback },
  agentName: string,
  prompt: string,
  options,
): Promise</* same outcome type as AgentManager.completeAsWithFallback today */ unknown>;
// AgentManager.completeAsWithFallback(agentName, prompt, options) keeps its signature and delegates.
```

**Goal** — Park only the failing provider on a native `fail-auth` (keyed by provider, stamped with the failing credential fingerprint), and release that cooldown automatically at the start of every agent call once the credential's fingerprint has changed.

**Motivation** — `fail-auth` has `cooldown: "run"` and `cooldownScope: "agent"`, so one provider's bad login retires every native tier for the rest of the run and a mid-run re-login can never bring native back (#2529).

**Approach** (verbatim from spec)

Size/complexity budget: `manager.ts` is at exactly 600 lines (source limit, no baseline entry; net growth fails `check:file-sizes`). `complexity-baseline.json` pins `runWithFallback` at 52 and `completeWithFallback` at 40; a higher score fails `check:complexity`. So the logic lives in new modules and `AgentManager`'s new members are one-line delegations to an `AuthCoordinator` in `auth-cooldown.ts`. The pinned functions gain only plain calls, with no new `if`, ternary, or `&&` / `||` / `??`. Biome's cognitive complexity does not count `?.` or `await`.

Symbols only READ: `providerOf(modelId?: string): string | undefined` (`packages/nax/src/agents/cooldown-provider.ts`; returns the first non-`unknown` path segment followed by a model segment, so `openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol` gives `openai-codex`, `unknown/sonnet` gives `undefined`). `servedAuth(providerId): AuthStamp | undefined` and `naxCredentialStore()` from `@nathapp/nax-agent`; `AuthStamp` is `{ fingerprint, source, account? }`. The native client reads `naxCredentialStore()` before each request, so after a `fail-auth` `servedAuth(P)` is the identity that failed; it is `undefined` only when the read itself failed. An OAuth fingerprint is computed from the refresh token, so a re-login changes it. Under `auth.onChange: "refuse"`, a read of a changed credential throws. `DEFAULT_AGENT_NAME = "native"` (`packages/nax/src/config/agent-defaults.ts`).

Symbols CHANGED (the baseline only locates the code; it is never the interface to implement):

`CooldownStore` (`packages/nax/src/agents/cooldown-store.ts`). Baseline: `mark(agent, failure, tier?, modelId?)`; `CooldownEntry` is `{ failure, expiresAt }`. Target: `mark(agent, failure, tier?, modelId?, auth?: AuthCooldownStamp)`, where `AuthCooldownStamp = { providerId: string; fingerprint?: string }` (imported with `import type`). `CooldownEntry` gains `auth?: AuthCooldownStamp`. With `auth`, the entry is keyed `<agent>::provider::<auth.providerId>`. Without it, keying is exactly today's, so the `fail-auth` policy row stays `cooldownScope: "agent"` and ACP agents and every other outcome keep their current keys. `_live`: when the probe names no tier and no model (the narrow key equals the agent), it also returns any live auth-stamped entry of that agent. This keeps today's agent-wide answer for identity-less probes, such as a flat `["native"]` fallback target. A probe that names a tier or model on another provider does not match the stamped entry. Two new methods: `authCooldowns(): readonly { key: string; agent: string; auth: AuthCooldownStamp }[]` lists live entries that carry `auth`; `release(key: string): boolean` deletes one entry and returns whether it existed.

`AgentManager` (`manager.ts`) and `IAgentManager` (`manager-types.ts`); each change is a delegation into the new module `packages/nax/src/agents/auth-cooldown.ts`:
- `markUnavailable(agent, reason, tier?, model?, authModel?)` gains an optional `authModel`, used only to resolve the stamp's provider and never as the cooldown key. It passes `authStampFor(agent, reason, authModel ?? modelId, deps)` as `mark`'s new `auth` argument; the `??` sits in `markUnavailable`, which is not ratcheted. `authStampFor` returns `{ providerId: P, fingerprint: deps.servedAuth(P)?.fingerprint }` for a `fail-auth` on agent `native` whose model id names provider P (by `providerOf`), and `undefined` otherwise.
- `refreshAuthCooldowns(): Promise<void>` (new). Delegates to `refreshAuthCooldowns(store, tracker, deps, logger)`: it checks each `authCooldowns()` entry (and, once US-003 lands, each provider that has a notice but no cooldown). For each, it awaits `deps.readCredential(providerId)` and then reads `deps.servedAuth(providerId)?.fingerprint`. When that is defined and differs from the recorded fingerprint (including when none was recorded), it calls `release(key)`. Only when `release` returns `true` does it log `info` on scope `agent-manager`: `Credential changed for <providerId> — <agent> available again`, with data `{ storyId: "_agent", agent, providerId, previousFingerprint, fingerprint }`. It also drops that provider's notice (the tracker's `drop(providerId)` can be a stub until US-003). A read that throws (logged at `debug`) or an unchanged fingerprint keeps the entry.
- `_agentManagerDeps` gains `servedAuth`, `readCredential(providerId): Promise<void>` (defaulting to `naxCredentialStore().read(providerId)`) and `listStoredProviders`. The auth module reaches the credential store only through these, so tests never touch the real `~/.nax`.
- `refreshAuthCooldowns` is added to `IAgentManager`.

`runWithFallback(input: RunFallbackInput)` (`manager-run-fallback.ts`). Baseline: calls `resolveStartAgent` first and calls `input.markUnavailable(...)` only on the swap path. Target: `RunFallbackInput` gains `refreshAuthCooldowns: () => Promise<void>`; `runWithFallback` awaits `input.refreshAuthCooldowns()` before `resolveStartAgent`. Both are plain calls. `AgentManager.runWithFallback` wires it.

`AgentManager.completeWithFallback`. Baseline: starts on `primaryAgent` without an availability probe; cooldowns only affect it through `nextCandidate` after a failure; on an availability failure it calls `this.markUnavailable(currentAgent, result.adapterFailure, currentTier, undefined)`. Target: it awaits `this.refreshAuthCooldowns()` before its loop, so `nextCandidate` sees released providers. Its `markUnavailable` call keeps `undefined` as the model, so cooldown keys on the complete path are unchanged, and passes `authModelOf(hopOptions.modelDef)` as the new `authModel`. `authModelOf` (in `auth-cooldown.ts`) returns `<provider>/<model>` for a model def, or `undefined`.

`auth-cooldown.ts` holds `authStampFor`, `authModelOf`, `refreshAuthCooldowns`, and (US-003) the `AuthReloginTracker` class and an `AuthCoordinator` that owns the tracker and binds the store and deps. `AgentManager` holds one `AuthCoordinator`; its members are one-line delegations. The module never imports `manager.ts`; deps are passed in.

Order of checks at the start of every agent call: (1) `refreshAuthCooldowns` releases any `fail-auth` provider whose credential changed; (2) start selection (`resolveStartAgent`) and `nextCandidate` then see the updated store; (3) after a hop fails with `fail-auth`, `markUnavailable` records the cooldown with its fingerprint (swap path only).

Failure handling: credential read throws (exec helper failure or refused change under `onChange: "refuse"`) -> entry kept, one `debug` line, no throw. Current fingerprint equals recorded -> entry kept, nothing logged. No fingerprint recorded and a credential now resolves -> entry released and `available again` logged. Two concurrent refreshes releasing the same entry -> only the call whose `release` returned `true` logs. `fail-auth` on a non-native agent or with no provider in the model id -> today's cooldown and key, no fingerprint, never released by refresh. Accepted trade-offs: an OAuth refresh-token rotation changes the fingerprint without a re-login (one more failing call, then cooldown returns); under `onChange: "refuse"` recovery never happens in that run.

Verification: `check:file-sizes` keeps `src/agents/manager.ts` at or below 600 lines, and `check:complexity` passes with the `runWithFallback` and `completeWithFallback` baselines unchanged.

**Scope** — In: CooldownStore stamp/keying/`_live` rule/`authCooldowns`/`release`; `auth-cooldown.ts` with `authStampFor`, `authModelOf`, `refreshAuthCooldowns`, `AuthCooldownStamp` (tracker `drop` may be a stub); the three `_agentManagerDeps` members; `markUnavailable` stamp; `refreshAuthCooldowns` on `AgentManager`/`IAgentManager`; calls at the start of `runWithFallback` and `completeWithFallback`; no-op `refreshAuthCooldowns` in the mock and fake agent managers. Out: `reportAuthFailure`, `authRelogins`, hint text, notices, run-end footer (US-003); start-line preview (US-004); recovering ACP agents; any change to the `fail-auth` policy row.

**Interface**
```ts
export type AuthCooldownStamp = { providerId: string; fingerprint?: string };
// CooldownStore
mark(agent, failure, tier?, modelId?, auth?: AuthCooldownStamp): void;
authCooldowns(): readonly { key: string; agent: string; auth: AuthCooldownStamp }[];
release(key: string): boolean;
// AgentManager / IAgentManager
markUnavailable(agent, reason, tier?, model?, authModel?): void;
refreshAuthCooldowns(): Promise<void>;
// auth-cooldown.ts
authStampFor(agent, reason, modelId, deps): AuthCooldownStamp | undefined;
authModelOf(modelDef): string | undefined; // `<provider>/<model>`
refreshAuthCooldowns(store, tracker, deps, logger): Promise<void>;
// RunFallbackInput
refreshAuthCooldowns: () => Promise<void>;
```

**Goal** — On a native `fail-auth`, log one `warn` naming the provider and the `nax auth login <provider>` fix (including when no fallback is configured), keep it as a notice, and repeat any still-relevant notice in the end-of-run summary.

**Motivation** — Nothing in the run log or summary pointed at `nax auth login openai-codex`, the actual fix, and `nax auth list` still showed an expiry a week away (#2528).

**Approach** (verbatim from spec)

`packages/nax/src/agents/auth-relogin.ts` holds the pure hint text and the notice type:
```ts
export interface AuthReloginNotice {
  readonly providerId: string;
  readonly agent: string;
  readonly message: string;
}

/** `credential` is the stored entry for `providerId`, or undefined when none is stored (env var, exec helper). */
export function authReloginHint(providerId: string, credential: StoredEntry | undefined, now: number): string {
  if (credential?.kind === "oauth" && credential.expires !== undefined && credential.expires > now)
    return `Login for ${providerId} was rejected before its recorded expiry (expires ${new Date(credential.expires).toISOString()}). Run: nax auth login ${providerId}`;
  if (credential?.kind === "oauth") return `Login for ${providerId} was rejected. Run: nax auth login ${providerId}`;
  if (credential?.kind === "api-key")
    return `API key for ${providerId} was rejected. Check or replace it: nax auth login ${providerId} (or nax auth import)`;
  return `Credentials for ${providerId} were rejected. Check where they come from with: nax auth list`;
}
```
`StoredEntry` is `{ providerId, kind: "api-key" | "oauth", expires?: number }` from `listStoredProviders(): Promise<StoredEntry[]>` (`@nathapp/nax-agent`).

`packages/nax/src/agents/auth-cooldown.ts` (created by US-002) is extended with the `AuthReloginTracker` class (claimed keys, notices by provider with their fingerprints, `report`, `notices`, `drop(providerId)`, `clear`) and an `AuthCoordinator` that owns the tracker and binds the store and deps. `AgentManager` holds one `AuthCoordinator`; `refreshAuthCooldowns`, `reportAuthFailure` and `authRelogins` are one-line delegations to it. It never imports `manager.ts`.

`AgentManager` / `IAgentManager` additions:
- `reportAuthFailure(agent, failure: AdapterFailure | undefined, tier?, model?): Promise<void>` (new). Delegates to `AuthReloginTracker.report(...)`. It returns at once unless `failure?.outcome` is `fail-auth`, the agent is `native`, and the model id (resolved as `markUnavailable` resolves it, through the fallback identity) names provider P. Synchronously, before any `await`, it claims the key `P` plus `deps.servedAuth(P)?.fingerprint`. A key already claimed returns without logging, so concurrent callers log once. It then awaits `deps.listStoredProviders()`, builds the text with `authReloginHint`, and logs it once as a `warn` on scope `agent-manager`, with data `{ storyId: "_agent", agent, providerId, kind?, expires? }`. It stores the notice, with its fingerprint, under P, replacing any earlier notice for P.
- `authRelogins(): readonly AuthReloginNotice[]` (new). Returns the stored notices, one per provider, in first-report order. A provider's notice is removed when `refreshAuthCooldowns` releases that provider.
- `reset()` also clears the tracker's notices and claimed keys.
- `refreshAuthCooldowns` (US-002) now also checks each provider that has a notice but no cooldown (a declined-swap failure); a notice with no cooldown is dropped, without the `available again` log line, when its recorded fingerprint differs from the current one. A released provider's notice is dropped.

`runWithFallback(input: RunFallbackInput)` (`manager-run-fallback.ts`). `RunFallbackInput` gains `reportAuthFailure: (agent, failure, tier?, model?) => Promise<void>`. It makes one plain call, `await input.reportAuthFailure(currentAgent, result.adapterFailure, currentHopKind.tier, dispatchedModel)`, between the same-agent retry block and the `request.noFallback` check. That single call covers the `noFallback` return, every exit of the `!swap.swap` block (fallback disabled, the hop cap), and the swap path. The claimed key dedupes a repeat. The `fail-auth` test lives inside `reportAuthFailure`. `AgentManager.runWithFallback` wires it.

`AgentManager.completeWithFallback`: one plain call, `await this.reportAuthFailure(currentAgent, result.adapterFailure, currentTier, authModelOf(hopOptions.modelDef))`, immediately before its `decideSwap` call. That covers both the declined branch and the swap path.

`RunSummary` (`packages/nax/src/log-format/types.ts`), `RunFooterOptions` (`packages/nax/src/execution/lifecycle/headless-formatter.ts`) and `formatRunSummary` (`packages/nax/src/log-format/summary.ts`): both types gain `authRelogins?: readonly string[]`. `outputRunFooter` copies it into the `RunSummary`. In non-json modes, `formatRunSummary` prints a `Re-login needed:` line after the counts block, followed by one indented line per hint, when the array is non-empty.

`runCompletionPhase(options)` (`packages/nax/src/execution/runner-completion.ts`): after `finishRun` returns (`finishRun` dispatches agents, so notices can still arrive during it), it calls `reportAuthRelogins(options.agentManager, logger)` from the new module `packages/nax/src/execution/auth-relogin-report.ts`. That helper reads `agentManager?.authRelogins()` once, logs each message as a `warn` on scope `execution` with data `{ storyId: "_run", providerId }` whatever the formatter mode, and returns the messages. `runCompletionPhase` passes the returned array as `authRelogins` to its existing `outputRunFooter` call.

Order of checks: after a hop fails with `fail-auth`, `markUnavailable` records the cooldown (swap path only); `reportAuthFailure` builds the hint from one call placed before the swap decision, so it runs whether or not the swap happens.

Failure handling: two concurrent `reportAuthFailure` calls for the same provider and fingerprint -> one `warn`. `listStoredProviders` throws -> hint built with no stored entry (the `nax auth list` wording), still one `warn`. `fail-auth` on a non-native agent or with no provider in the model id -> no hint. `fail-auth` whose swap is declined (no fallback, hop cap) -> hint logged and kept as a notice, no cooldown (as today); a later credential change drops the notice. A released provider that fails again -> a new cooldown with the new fingerprint; the claimed key differs, so the hint is logged again and replaces the notice.

Verification: `check:file-sizes` keeps `src/agents/manager.ts` at or below 600 lines and `src/execution/runner-completion.ts` at or below 600, and `check:complexity` passes with all three pinned baselines (`runWithFallback` 52, `completeWithFallback` 40, `runCompletionPhase` 34) unchanged. The new call sites add plain calls only - no new `if`, ternary, `&&`/`||`/`??`.

**Scope** — In: `auth-relogin.ts`; tracker/coordinator in `auth-cooldown.ts`; `reportAuthFailure`/`authRelogins` on `AgentManager` and `IAgentManager`; `reset()` clearing; the two call sites; `auth-relogin-report.ts`; `runCompletionPhase` wiring; `RunSummary`/`RunFooterOptions`/`formatRunSummary` footer block; mock/fake agent manager members. Out: the cooldown stamp, release and refresh mechanism (US-002); start-line preview (US-004); recording a cooldown when a swap is declined; a TUI hint.

**Interface**
```ts
export interface AuthReloginNotice { readonly providerId: string; readonly agent: string; readonly message: string }
export function authReloginHint(providerId: string, credential: StoredEntry | undefined, now: number): string;
reportAuthFailure(agent, failure: AdapterFailure | undefined, tier?, model?): Promise<void>;
authRelogins(): readonly AuthReloginNotice[];
export function reportAuthRelogins(agentManager: IAgentManager | undefined, logger): readonly string[]; // execution/auth-relogin-report.ts
// RunSummary and RunFooterOptions: authRelogins?: readonly string[]
```
